    {
      "reference": "skills/author-preflight.md@2",
      "path": "skills/author-preflight.md",
      "digest": "sha256:189d31acb7da3ff8f3bdb13efd6ade0d4c832b1bb5c9e1b97b7e679776046824",
      "content": "---\nid: author-preflight\nversion: 2\n---\n\n# Ephemeral author preflight\n\nBefore proposing authored Lifecycle Data, inspect the proposal in isolation using\nthe applicable independent-Review rubric and the exact Assignment packet.\n\n1. Check contract validity, exact traceability, information allocation, scope,\n   internal consistency, verifiability, and proportionality.\n2. Treat a concern as blocking only when you can name the exact violated\n   criterion, concrete evidence or counterexample, and the material consequence\n   that prevents safe use. Correct every such concern before publication.\n3. Correct clear `needs-triage` concerns when they are local and authorized.\n   Do not expand scope to satisfy advisory preferences.\n4. For a correction, compare the proposal with the exact prior Revision. Name\n   each behavior or commitment added, removed, strengthened, or deferred, and\n   trace every change to a blocking finding or exact authority. Prefer the\n   smallest local delta that resolves the finding. Explicitly compare a bounded\n   correction, deferral or removal, and retention of broader behavior; do not\n   infer stakeholder need from determinism, prior elaboration, or an agent\n   proposal. If safe correction requires a new stakeholder-visible choice that\n   is not supplied, stop rather than elaborate.\n5. For a definition set, perform a deletion/merge pass and confirm every split is\n   justified by distinct behavior, responsibility, boundary, risk, trust context,\n   or verification need.\n\nThis preflight is bounded working memory, not Lifecycle Data. Publish no REV,\nFinding, score, transcript, or telemetry from it. Do not place preflight content\nin the response or Review Context, and do not supply it to the independent\nreviewer. The later independent Review remains fresh-eyed and authoritative.\n"
    },
    {
      "reference": "policies/rubrics/bootstrap-review.md@3",
      "path": "policies/rubrics/bootstrap-review.md",
      "digest": "sha256:43e0a610b45f0212f1ed6dd6a49363c68764a92c406c84854aabb003fae572f3",
      "content": "---\nid: bootstrap-review\nversion: 3\n---\n\n# Bootstrap review policy\n\n## Applicability\n\nEvery PSP, STK, and SYS revision is reviewed individually. Every frozen candidate\nBSL is reviewed; review-context and accepted BSLs are not. A DEC is reviewed when\nit changes scope, waives or defers work, disposes of a datum, or signs off a gate.\nREV and QST do not require their own REV.\n\n## Universal rubric\n\nA passing subject must have valid identity, schema, provenance, and links; no\nprimary blocking findings; clear and internally consistent content; fidelity to\nits exact parents and applicable decisions; no unjustified scope; and no hidden\nunresolved question.\n\n## Type checks\n\n- **PSP:** coherent problem, users, goals, non-goals, scope, constraints, and\n  measurable success without premature implementation detail.\n- **STK:** one stakeholder-visible commitment, verification intent, rationale,\n  and exact PSP derivation; no prescribed design.\n- **SYS:** one solution-independent behavior or constraint, verification intent,\n  rationale, authorized STK derivation, and no coverage duplication.\n- **BSL candidate:** exact complete membership, separated evidence, valid hashes\n  and resolved links, complete member reviews, coherent role/scope, and no\n  blocking QST.\n- **DEC:** alternatives and rationale proportionate to consequence, exact scope,\n  valid evidence links, and no authority beyond the recorded decision.\n\n## Findings and outcome\n\nFindings target exact revisions. A finding is `blocking` only when all five\nconditions hold: it names an exact applicable criterion; supplies concrete\nevidence or a reproducible counterexample; states a material consequence; stays\nwithin the target and authorized scope; and shows why the target cannot be used\nsafely without correction. Material ambiguity additionally requires two plausible\ninterpretations whose difference changes observable behavior, safety, authority,\nor verification. Every blocking finding records `criterion`, `evidence`, and\n`material_consequence` explicitly. Only an unresolved primary blocker controls\nthe outcome of the subject under Review; a blocker owned by an exact collateral\ntarget is routed to that target without failing an otherwise usable subject.\n\nUse `needs-triage` when a usable subject has a collateral or owner-dependent\nconcern. Use `advisory` for non-blocking improvement. Style preference, optional\nclarity, or a speculative future risk cannot fail a Review. Any unresolved\nprimary blocking finding requires `fail`.\n\nEvery failed Review classifies its complete blocking finding set. Use\n`correction_authority: stakeholder` if any blocker requires a new choice or\nchange to stakeholder-owned behavior, scope, priority, deferral, removal,\nretention, or tradeoffs. Use `correction_authority: package-evidence` only when\nthe exact supplied package evidence determines the complete correction without\nsuch a choice. Omission is invalid; it never defaults to autonomous correction.\nPassing and cancelled Reviews do not carry a correction-authority\nclassification. A cancelled Review gives no reusable evidence. Completed REVs\nare immutable.\n"
    }
  ],
  "exactInputs": [
    {
      "inputs": [
        {
          "name": "subject",
          "contract": {
            "types": [
              "MAP",
              "PSP",
              "STK"
            ],
            "cardinality": "one",
            "identity": "revision"
          },
          "values": [
            {
              "identity": {
                "id": "PSP-6WE7NE0F46",
                "revision_id": "PSP-6WE7NE0F46-r00004",
                "type": "PSP",
                "revision": 4
              },
              "data": {
                "id": "PSP-6WE7NE0F46",
                "revision": 4,
                "revision_id": "PSP-6WE7NE0F46-r00004",
                "type": "PSP",
                "payload": {
                  "title": "iso-date-weekday product specification",
                  "rationale": "A strict, bounded, noninteractive civil-date counter provides the smallest product surface that gives release, migration, test, audit, and public-pilot workflows deterministic weekday distributions, atomic validation, stable automation bytes, and independent black-box verification without introducing broader calendar, input, or presentation capabilities.",
                  "problem": "Release, test, migration, and audit workflows receive lists of civil dates and need reproducible weekday distributions, while host date libraries may vary in leniency, locale and timezone defaults, year interpretation, and normalization of impossible dates. They need a fail-closed product that treats dates as exact civil-date records, validates all input before reporting, and produces byte-stable results independent of locale, timezone, clock, and environment.",
                  "users": [
                    "Release and CI engineers checking date-indexed manifests.",
                    "Data and migration engineers validating bounded batches before downstream work.",
                    "Test and assurance engineers comparing exact success and failure bytes across canonical and malformed fixtures.",
                    "Maintainers of public pilot evidence requiring reproducible black-box behavior."
                  ],
                  "goals": [
                    "Accept exactly one explicit regular file containing at least one canonical proleptic-Gregorian civil date and produce deterministic Monday-through-Sunday weekday counts.",
                    "Validate record framing, encoding, canonical date syntax, calendar validity, and declared bounds fail-closed before emitting any success bytes.",
                    "Provide byte-stable success, help, and failure behavior suitable for automation and independent black-box verification.",
                    "Remain a bounded, noninteractive local CLI whose results do not depend on host date-library coercion, locale, timezone, clock, network, or environment."
                  ],
                  "non_goals": [
                    "Standard input, multiple files, directories, recursion, or globbing.",
                    "Output files, sorting, per-date output, percentages, locale weekday names, or configurable weekday keys.",
                    "Calendar selection, timezone conversion, timestamp parsing, date normalization, recovery after an invalid record, or general date parsing.",
                    "Comments, blank-line skipping, whitespace trimming, Unicode normalization, network access, or host-date-library coercion.",
                    "A network service or calendar application."
                  ],
                  "workflows": [
                    "A user runs `iso-date-weekday <file>` with exactly one file operand; after the complete file is accepted, the CLI emits one compact JSON weekday-count object and succeeds.",
                    "A user supplies a malformed, impossible, unreadable, or oversized input, or invalid invocation; the CLI emits no success bytes, reports exactly one stable failure, and exits nonzero according to the declared precedence.",
                    "A user invokes `iso-date-weekday --help`; the CLI performs no file I/O, writes exactly the 31 UTF-8 bytes `Usage: iso-date-weekday <file>\\n` to stdout, writes zero bytes to stderr, exits with status 0, and emits nothing else.",
                    "Test and assurance users repeatedly execute canonical and malformed fixtures, byte-compare outputs, and check weekday results against an implementation-independent oracle or fixed trusted vectors."
                  ],
                  "success_measures": [
                    "Success fixtures cover every weekday, repeated dates, mixed ordering, leap-day boundaries, century exceptions, dates `0001-01-01` and `9999-12-31`, and both accepted final-LF forms; repeated runs byte-match the counts and member order.",
                    "Trusted weekday checks include `0001-01-01` as Mon, `1970-01-01` as Thu, `2000-02-29` as Tue, and `2024-02-29` as Thu, using an implementation-independent oracle or fixed trusted vectors rather than merely repeating the product algorithm.",
                    "Rejection fixtures cover empty input; leading, interior, and repeated blank lines; CRLF and bare CR; spaces and tabs; a BOM at the beginning or within input; malformed UTF-8; non-ASCII text; wrong widths; alternate punctuation; year `0000`; invalid months and days; `1900-02-29`; timestamps; signs; and extra fields. Every rejection proves empty stdout and no partial counts.",
                    "Boundary checks prove that accepted files contain no more than 1,000,000 records and that the input-byte maximum is 11,000,011 bytes, including LF separators and an optional final LF. A valid 1,000,001-record file occupies 11,000,010 bytes without a final LF or 11,000,011 bytes with one and yields `count-overflow` after complete framing and date validation; bytes beyond 11,000,011 yield `input-too-large` first. Arithmetic overflow while counting also yields `count-overflow`, although the record bound prevents it for conforming accepted input.",
                    "Usage checks prove zero operands, multiple operands, and unsupported options fail before file I/O, while `iso-date-weekday --help` performs no file I/O, writes exactly the 31 UTF-8 bytes `Usage: iso-date-weekday <file>\\n` to stdout with one terminal LF byte (`0x0A`), writes zero bytes to stderr, exits with status 0, and emits nothing else.",
                    "I/O and mutation checks prove that missing, unreadable, directory, interrupted-read, and unexpected read failures disclose neither paths nor host messages and yield the stable `io-error`; repeated execution is independent of locale, timezone, current date, network, and environment variables. For the same readable operand bytes and effective readability, unrelated directory activity, metadata changes that do not affect access, and replacement or mutation elsewhere do not change observable behavior. If the explicit operand changes while being read, checks prove that the CLI applies its contract to the actual bytes obtained by one complete read: valid complete bytes produce their weekday counts, invalid complete bytes produce the corresponding deterministic validation failure, and mutation that prevents or interrupts a complete read or makes the operand inaccessible produces `io-error`. No check requires snapshot isolation, locking, retries, or preservation of a result for bytes that existed before mutation."
                  ],
                  "scope": [
                    "A deterministic bounded CLI named `iso-date-weekday`.",
                    "Exactly one explicit regular UTF-8 file containing one canonical `YYYY-MM-DD` record per line and at least one date.",
                    "Proleptic-Gregorian years `0001` through `9999`, with civil-date-only weekday computation.",
                    "Compact JSON counts ordered Monday through Sunday, with atomic fail-closed validation and stable automation output.",
                    "A PUBLIC, initially empty pilot target at `https://github.com/taylorrowser/mdlm-iso-date-weekday-pilot`."
                  ],
                  "constraints": [
                    "The normative initial source boundary is repository file `LANE-IDENTITY.md`, Git blob `7c12a15b476ef1a54b48572f3f8a616e8b1dc968`, exact SHA-256 `743f2c2ba8a5648bb8dc34396cb4ab36b401d5dc7842b9ccec4b49d5724573af`.",
                    "Each record is exactly ten ASCII bytes in `YYYY-MM-DD` form. Month and day are exactly two decimal digits; Gregorian validity includes leap years divisible by 4 except centuries not divisible by 400.",
                    "Records are separated by one LF byte. One final LF is an accepted terminator and does not create a blank record. Empty input, a leading LF, two adjacent LFs, or any other empty record is a blank-line error.",
                    "CRLF, bare CR, surrounding whitespace, alternate separators, shortened fields, signs, year zero, ordinal dates, week dates, timestamps, timezone suffixes, Unicode digits, impossible dates, a UTF-8 BOM anywhere, invalid UTF-8, and non-ASCII bytes are rejected without normalization.",
                    "On success, stdout is exactly one compact JSON object with integer members ordered `Mon`, `Tue`, `Wed`, `Thu`, `Fri`, `Sat`, `Sun`, followed by one LF; stderr is empty and exit status is 0.",
                    "No success bytes are emitted until the entire file is read and accepted. Any usage, I/O, encoding, blank-line, date, input-bound, or count-overflow failure leaves stdout empty, writes one stable compact JSON error plus LF to stderr, and uses a declared nonzero exit status.",
                    "Failure precedence is usage, open/read failure, input-byte bound, UTF-8/BOM and record framing, canonical date validity, then record/count overflow; one detected failure produces exactly one error object.",
                    "Output-producing input is limited to at most 1,000,000 date records. The input-byte maximum is 11,000,011 bytes, including separators and an optional final LF. Reading stops after enough bytes to distinguish oversize input: bytes beyond 11,000,011 yield `input-too-large`. After complete framing and canonical date validation, a valid 1,000,001st record yields `count-overflow`; otherwise no more than 1,000,000 records are accepted. Arithmetic overflow while counting is a defensive `count-overflow` failure, although the record bound prevents it for conforming accepted input.",
                    "Zero operands, multiple operands, and unsupported options fail before file I/O. `iso-date-weekday --help` performs no file I/O, writes exactly the 31 UTF-8 bytes `Usage: iso-date-weekday <file>\\n` to stdout, where the terminal `\\n` denotes one LF byte (`0x0A`), writes zero bytes to stderr, exits with status 0, and emits nothing else.",
                    "Missing, unreadable, directory, interrupted-read, and unexpected read failures do not disclose paths or host messages and produce the stable `io-error`. Behavior is independent of locale, timezone, current date, network, and environment variables. Filesystem mutations must leave observable behavior unchanged only when they leave the explicit operand's bytes and effective readability unchanged; unrelated directory activity, metadata changes that do not affect access, and replacement or mutation elsewhere must not affect the result for the same readable operand bytes. If the explicit operand changes while being read, the CLI applies its contract to the actual bytes obtained by one complete read: valid complete bytes produce their weekday counts; malformed, oversized, over-record, or otherwise invalid complete bytes produce the corresponding deterministic validation failure; and mutation that prevents or interrupts a complete read or makes the operand inaccessible produces `io-error`. The CLI does not promise snapshot isolation, locking, retries, or preservation of a result for bytes that existed before mutation."
                  ]
