                "body": "# iso-date-weekday product specification\n\n## Rationale\n\nA strict, bounded, noninteractive civil-date counter provides the smallest product surface that gives release, migration, test, audit, and public-pilot workflows deterministic weekday distributions, atomic validation, stable automation bytes, and independent black-box verification without introducing broader calendar, input, or presentation capabilities.\n\n## Problem\n\nRelease, test, migration, and audit workflows receive lists of civil dates and need reproducible weekday distributions, while host date libraries may vary in leniency, locale and timezone defaults, year interpretation, and normalization of impossible dates. They need a fail-closed product that treats dates as exact civil-date records, validates all input before reporting, and produces byte-stable results independent of locale, timezone, clock, and environment.\n\n## Users\n\n- Release and CI engineers checking date-indexed manifests.\n- Data and migration engineers validating bounded batches before downstream work.\n- Test and assurance engineers comparing exact success and failure bytes across canonical and malformed fixtures.\n- Maintainers of public pilot evidence requiring reproducible black-box behavior.\n\n## Goals\n\n- Accept exactly one explicit regular file containing at least one canonical proleptic-Gregorian civil date and produce deterministic Monday-through-Sunday weekday counts.\n- Validate record framing, encoding, canonical date syntax, calendar validity, and declared bounds fail-closed before emitting any success bytes.\n- Provide byte-stable success, help, and failure behavior suitable for automation and independent black-box verification.\n- Remain a bounded, noninteractive local CLI whose results do not depend on host date-library coercion, locale, timezone, clock, network, or environment.\n\n## Essential workflows\n\n1. A user runs `iso-date-weekday <file>` with exactly one file operand; after the complete file is accepted, the CLI emits one compact JSON weekday-count object and succeeds.\n2. A user supplies a malformed, impossible, unreadable, or oversized input, or invalid invocation; the CLI emits no success bytes, reports exactly one stable failure, and exits nonzero according to the declared precedence.\n3. A user invokes `--help`; the CLI performs no file I/O and writes one fixed usage line.\n4. Test and assurance users repeatedly execute canonical and malformed fixtures, byte-compare outputs, and check weekday results against an implementation-independent oracle or fixed trusted vectors.\n\n## Success measures\n\n- Success fixtures cover every weekday, repeated dates, mixed ordering, leap-day boundaries, century exceptions, dates `0001-01-01` and `9999-12-31`, and both accepted final-LF forms; repeated runs byte-match the counts and member order.\n- Trusted weekday checks include `0001-01-01` as Mon, `1970-01-01` as Thu, `2000-02-29` as Tue, and `2024-02-29` as Thu, using an implementation-independent oracle or fixed trusted vectors rather than merely repeating the product algorithm.\n- Rejection fixtures cover empty input; leading, interior, and repeated blank lines; CRLF and bare CR; spaces and tabs; a BOM at the beginning or within input; malformed UTF-8; non-ASCII text; wrong widths; alternate punctuation; year `0000`; invalid months and days; `1900-02-29`; timestamps; signs; and extra fields. Every rejection proves empty stdout and no partial counts.\n- Boundary checks prove acceptance of no more than 1,000,000 records and 11,000,000 input bytes, including LF separators and an optional final LF; a 1,000,001st record or arithmetic beyond the declared unsigned counter range yields `count-overflow`, while bytes beyond the byte bound yield `input-too-large`.\n- Usage checks prove zero operands, multiple operands, and unsupported options fail before file I/O, while `--help` performs no file I/O and writes its fixed usage line.\n- I/O failure checks prove missing, unreadable, directory, and unexpected read failures disclose neither paths nor host messages; repeated execution is independent of locale, timezone, current date, network, filesystem mutation, and environment variables.\n\n## Scope\n\n- A deterministic bounded CLI named `iso-date-weekday`.\n- Exactly one explicit regular UTF-8 file containing one canonical `YYYY-MM-DD` record per line and at least one date.\n- Proleptic-Gregorian years `0001` through `9999`, with civil-date-only weekday computation.\n- Compact JSON counts ordered Monday through Sunday, with atomic fail-closed validation and stable automation output.\n- A PUBLIC, initially empty pilot target at `https://github.com/taylorrowser/mdlm-iso-date-weekday-pilot`.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- The normative initial source boundary is repository file `LANE-IDENTITY.md`, Git blob `7c12a15b476ef1a54b48572f3f8a616e8b1dc968`, exact SHA-256 `743f2c2ba8a5648bb8dc34396cb4ab36b401d5dc7842b9ccec4b49d5724573af`.\n- Each record is exactly ten ASCII bytes in `YYYY-MM-DD` form. Month and day are exactly two decimal digits; Gregorian validity includes leap years divisible by 4 except centuries not divisible by 400.\n- Records are separated by one LF byte. One final LF is an accepted terminator and does not create a blank record. Empty input, a leading LF, two adjacent LFs, or any other empty record is a blank-line error.\n- CRLF, bare CR, surrounding whitespace, alternate separators, shortened fields, signs, year zero, ordinal dates, week dates, timestamps, timezone suffixes, Unicode digits, impossible dates, a UTF-8 BOM anywhere, invalid UTF-8, and non-ASCII bytes are rejected without normalization.\n- On success, stdout is exactly one compact JSON object with integer members ordered `Mon`, `Tue`, `Wed`, `Thu`, `Fri`, `Sat`, `Sun`, followed by one LF; stderr is empty and exit status is 0.\n- No success bytes are emitted until the entire file is read and accepted. Any usage, I/O, encoding, blank-line, date, input-bound, or count-overflow failure leaves stdout empty, writes one stable compact JSON error plus LF to stderr, and uses a declared nonzero exit status.\n- Failure precedence is usage, open/read failure, input-byte bound, UTF-8/BOM and record framing, canonical date validity, then record/count overflow; one detected failure produces exactly one error object.\n- Accepted input is limited to 1,000,000 date records and 11,000,000 bytes, including separators and an optional final LF. Reading stops after enough bytes to distinguish oversize input; a 1,000,001st record or arithmetic beyond the declared unsigned counter range is `count-overflow`, and excess bytes are `input-too-large`.\n- Zero operands, multiple operands, and unsupported options fail before file I/O. `--help` performs no file I/O and writes one fixed usage line.\n- Missing, unreadable, directory, and unexpected read failures do not disclose paths or host messages. Behavior is independent of locale, timezone, current date, network, filesystem mutation, and environment variables.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Standard input, multiple files, directories, recursion, or globbing.\n- Output files, sorting, per-date output, percentages, locale weekday names, or configurable weekday keys.\n- Calendar selection, timezone conversion, timestamp parsing, date normalization, recovery after an invalid record, or general date parsing.\n- Comments, blank-line skipping, whitespace trimming, Unicode normalization, network access, or host-date-library coercion.\n- A network service or calendar application."
