                "body": "# Independent contextual review of PSP-6WE7NE0F46-r00001\n\n**Review kind:** Contextual  \n**Rubric:** `policies/rubrics/bootstrap-review.md@3`  \n**Outcome:** Fail  \n**Correction authority:** Stakeholder\n\n## Findings\n\n### F-001 — Primary, blocking\n\n**Criterion:** The bootstrap universal rubric and PSP type check require clear, internally consistent content, measurable success, and no hidden unresolved question.\n\n**Evidence:** A file containing 1,000,001 canonical ten-byte records separated by LF and no final LF occupies 11,000,010 bytes. The PSP says that bytes beyond 11,000,000 produce `input-too-large` and places the byte-bound check before record/count overflow, but it also says that a 1,000,001st record produces `count-overflow`. Enforcing byte precedence yields `input-too-large` at byte 11,000,001; honoring the record-limit clause yields `count-overflow` after recognizing record 1,000,001. Those plausible interpretations have different observable failure bytes. The alternative trigger of arithmetic beyond a “declared unsigned counter range” is also undefined because no range is declared.\n\n**Material consequence:** The required boundary fixture has no single expected result, allowing independent implementations and verifiers to disagree while each follows part of the PSP. Deterministic downstream decomposition and byte-stable black-box verification are unsafe until the stakeholder selects one behavior or changes the limits or precedence. Clarify by choosing a precedence-consistent failure contract or removing/redefining the unreachable overflow branch."
              }
            }
          ],
