                },
                "body": "# Select iso-date-weekday as the intended product\n\n**Kind:** Scope  \n**Effective scope:** `QST-HXD7YTYKZM-r00002`\n\n## Decision\n\nThe selected product is `iso-date-weekday`, a deterministic bounded CLI that reads exactly one strict UTF-8 file containing canonical proleptic-Gregorian dates, one `YYYY-MM-DD` record per line, and emits stable compact JSON weekday counts in Monday-through-Sunday order. The normative initial source boundary is repository file `LANE-IDENTITY.md`, Git blob `7c12a15b476ef1a54b48572f3f8a616e8b1dc968`, exact SHA-256 `743f2c2ba8a5648bb8dc34396cb4ab36b401d5dc7842b9ccec4b49d5724573af`.\n\nProblem: release, test, migration, and audit workflows often receive lists of civil dates and need reproducible weekday distributions. Host date libraries can differ in leniency, locale defaults, timezone handling, year interpretation, and normalization of impossible dates. This product supplies one small fail-closed contract that treats dates as exact civil-date records, validates the complete input before reporting anything, and produces byte-stable counts independent of locale, timezone, clock, and environment.\n\nConcrete users and stakeholders are: (1) release and CI engineers checking date-indexed manifests; (2) data and migration engineers validating bounded batches before downstream work; (3) test and assurance engineers comparing exact success and failure bytes across canonical and malformed fixtures; and (4) maintainers of the public pilot evidence who need reproducible black-box behavior. It is a noninteractive local CLI, not a network service, calendar application, timezone converter, or general date parser.\n\nGoals and essential workflows:\n1. A user runs `iso-date-weekday <file>` with exactly one file operand. For valid input the command emits exactly one compact JSON object with integer members in this fixed order: `Mon`, `Tue`, `Wed`, `Thu`, `Fri`, `Sat`, `Sun`, followed by one LF; stderr is empty and exit status is 0.\n2. Each record is exactly ten ASCII bytes in `YYYY-MM-DD` form. Years are `0001` through `9999`; month and day are exactly two decimal digits; validity follows the proleptic Gregorian calendar, including leap years divisible by 4 except centuries not divisible by 400.\n3. Records are separated by one LF byte. A single final LF is accepted as a terminator and does not create a blank record. At least one date is required. Empty input, a leading LF, two adjacent LFs, or any otherwise empty record is rejected as a blank-line error.\n4. The CLI accepts neither CRLF nor bare CR, whitespace around a record, alternate separators, shortened fields, signs, year zero, ordinal dates, week dates, timestamps, timezone suffixes, Unicode digits, or normalization of impossible dates. A UTF-8 BOM anywhere is rejected. Invalid UTF-8, non-ASCII bytes, and noncanonical or impossible dates are rejected.\n5. Weekdays are computed from the civil date alone, without host timezone or locale behavior. Representative anchors include `1970-01-01` as Thu, `2000-02-29` as Tue, `2024-02-29` as Thu, and `0001-01-01` as Mon.\n6. Validation and counting are atomic: no success bytes are emitted until the entire file is read and accepted. Any usage, I/O, encoding, blank-line, date, input-bound, or count-overflow failure emits empty stdout, one stable compact JSON error plus LF on stderr, and a declared nonzero exit status.\n\nMeasurable success:\n- Exact black-box fixtures cover each weekday, repeated dates, mixed ordering, leap-day boundaries, century exceptions, the minimum and maximum supported dates, and both accepted final-LF forms. Counts and member order are byte-compared across repeated runs.\n- Rejection fixtures cover empty input; leading, interior, and repeated blank lines; CRLF and bare CR; spaces and tabs; BOM at the beginning and within input; malformed UTF-8; non-ASCII text; wrong widths; alternate punctuation; year `0000`; invalid months and days; `1900-02-29`; timestamps; signs; and extra fields. Every case proves empty stdout and no partial counts.\n- The accepted input is bounded to at most 1,000,000 date records and at most 11,000,000 input bytes, including LF separators and an optional final LF. Reading stops after enough bytes to distinguish an oversize input. A 1,000,001st record or arithmetic count beyond the declared unsigned counter range is a stable `count-overflow` failure; input bytes beyond the byte bound are a stable `input-too-large` failure.\n- Usage tests prove zero operands, multiple operands, and unsupported options fail before file I/O; `--help` performs no file I/O and writes one fixed usage line.\n- Missing, unreadable, directory, or unexpected read failures do not disclose paths or host messages. Repeated execution is independent of locale, timezone, current date, network, filesystem mutation, and environment variables.\n- Verification includes an implementation-independent weekday oracle or fixed trusted vectors rather than merely repeating the product algorithm.\n\nError precedence is usage, open/read failure, input-byte bound, UTF-8/BOM and record framing, canonical date validity, then record/count overflow. For a single detected failure the output is exactly one error object; rejected input never emits a partial success object. The public pilot target is `https://github.com/taylorrowser/mdlm-iso-date-weekday-pilot`, selected PUBLIC and initially empty.\n\nScope boundaries and exclusions: one explicit regular file only; no standard input, multiple files, directories, recursion, globbing, output files, sorting, per-date output, percentages, locale names, configurable weekday keys, calendar selection, timezone conversion, timestamp parsing, date normalization, recovery after an invalid record, comments, blank-line skipping, whitespace trimming, Unicode normalization, network access, or host-date-library coercion. Do not expand these boundaries without explicit later Lifecycle Data.\n\n## Rationale\n\nThe attended stakeholder selected and bounded the intended product. The strict one-file civil-date counter is selected because it is the smallest surface that jointly satisfies deterministic weekday distribution, bounded atomic validation, stable automation output, and independent black-box verification. The rejected alternatives either weaken determinism and fail-closed behavior or add unrelated calendar, input, and presentation surfaces. Keeping the byte and record bounds, error precedence, exact output, public pilot target, and all existing boundaries in the reviewed product scope makes atomicity, overflow behavior, fixture identity, and reproducibility auditable before implementation. This correction addresses `REV-YHCD3F9479-r00001` finding `F-001` without changing the attended answer and requires a fresh independent Review of the replacement.\n\n## Alternatives\n\n1. A permissive host-library date parser accepting normalized dates, locale or timezone defaults, and noncanonical forms. Rejected because cross-runtime coercion and normalization conflict with fail-closed byte-reproducible civil-date semantics.\n2. Standard-input or multi-file streaming instead of exactly one explicit file. Rejected because it adds framing, ordering, and I/O surfaces without serving the bounded manifest-checking pilot, and weakens reproducible fixture identity.\n3. A broader calendar or timestamp tool supporting timezones, locale weekday names, configurable calendars, per-record output, or normalization. Rejected because those are distinct products with substantially larger requirements and verification surfaces.\n4. A narrower checker that only validates dates or reports one weekday per date. Rejected because it does not satisfy the selected weekday-distribution workflow and either omits the required counts or emits a larger order-sensitive stream.\n5. Deferring byte/record bounds, error precedence, and exact output until implementation. Rejected because those choices materially affect atomicity, overflow behavior, and black-box reproducibility and therefore belong in the reviewed product boundary."
              }
            }
          ],
